    'eyes-talmok-reckless-1103': { pri: 2, group: G1, title: 'The Talmok swings recklessly at its own turn\'s start; its rage halves the next blow, not the one that sets it off (11-03)', pt: 'the grid\'s rules, the desk\'s read of his Hex play record 11-03 ("adv: reckless target" in round 1 before the Talmok had acted; "12 bludgeoning ... rages! ... resists bludgeoning: 12 > 6") (deep16/js/rules.js RU.startTurn, RU.edges; deep16/js/battle.js hurt) · eyes',
      obj: 'Take Talmok\'s bout on the grid and ride out its reckless swings — do they start only on its own turn?',
      look: 'Barley (level 4) against the Talmok, up the screen. Round 1, before the Talmok has taken a turn, Barley\'s blow at him has no advantage (the roll reads no "reckless target"). At the Talmok\'s own turn\'s start the card reads "Talmok swings recklessly. (advantage on its blows; blows at it have it too, till its next turn)": its blows that turn have advantage, and so has every attack roll at him until that turn\'s start comes round again; an opportunity attack of his on your turn has no advantage (the edge is his own turn\'s only). The first hit on him: "roars and rages!" -- that blow lands whole; the next blow of blade or fist is the one halved ("resists"). A barbarian\'s Reckless Attack (RECKLESS on its ring) is the same: advantage on its STR swings that turn, and at it till its next turn. The Hex\'s Talmok bout plays the same.',
      url: 'deep16/?npc=talmok&vs=barley:4&lvl=4&fresh=1103h' },
    'eyes-grokslash-1031': { pri: 2, group: G1, title: 'Barley swings a longsword: free Grok skinned over the mannequin, cut with the sword drawn in (a test sheet, 10-31)', pt: 'the bestiary §52, the mannequin seat, his "should we have a cutter try put a longsword in its hand and cut it (without overwriting anything we actually use?)" (tools/barley-grokslash-sheet.py; deep16/art-src/mannequin/README.md, the score) · eyes',
      obj: 'Watch Barley swing a longsword, an animation no fight uses — does it read as a cut, not a punch?',
      look: 'The SLASH row plays S, SE and E (and SW, W from them, the sword in his left hand there by the mirror rule); NE and N sit apart in SLASHBACK, kept out of the row till your word. Six frames: guard, the sword raised, raised higher, THE HIT on 4 (the arm out, the blade at the foe), then the fists back down -- Grok drew a punch, not a cut, so the follow-through has the sword low at his hip. barley_p3 idle beside each row for his size. Nothing in the game plays this sheet.',
      url: 'tools/sheet-play.html?sheet=barley_grokslash_p1&ref=barley_p3&face=0,7,6,5,4&z=3' },
